Generics gotchas — arrays and casts.

Most generics friction traces back to one clash: arrays and generics have opposite designs. Arrays are covariant and reified — they carry their type at run time. Generics are invariant and erased — they don't. Mixing them produces the warnings and errors that make generics feel fiddly. And knowing the root cause makes each one obvious.

Start with the fact that arrays are covariant — and that it bites at run time. Unlike generics, a string array is an object array. That feels convenient, but it defers a type error from compile time to run time. So you can assign a new string array to an object-array variable — that's legal, because arrays are covariant. But then you store forty-two into slot zero, and it compiles — yet it throws an array-store exception at run time. The array remembers that it's really a string array, because it's reified, and it throws when you try to insert an integer. Generics deliberately went the other way. They're invariant, so the analogous mistake — assigning a list of string to a list of object — is a compile error, caught early. The two models are opposites by design.

That's why you can't create a generic array. Because generics are erased, but arrays check their type at run time, new-T-array, and new-list-of-string-array, are both compile errors — "generic array creation". If they were allowed, the array's run-time check would have nothing to check against, because the element type was erased, so the covariant-store protection would silently break. Java forbids the creation, rather than let that hole open. The workarounds you'll actually use: inside a generic class, back it with an object array and cast on the way out — cast a new object array of size n to a T array — which is exactly what array-list does, and it draws an unchecked warning, but it's safe because it never escapes. Or, avoid arrays entirely, and use a list of T — which is usually the right answer. The honest guidance is: prefer a list of T to a T array. Generic collections sidestep the whole problem; reach for arrays only for primitives, or a measured performance need.

Now, unchecked warnings — what they mean, and what to do. An unchecked warning is the compiler saying "this cast involves an erased type, so I can't guarantee it's safe — you're vouching for it." It appears when you cast a raw list to a list of string, and similar. Don't ignore them blindly, and don't blanket-suppress them. When you've proven a specific cast is safe, suppress it on the narrowest scope — ideally a single local variable or method — with a comment saying why. So you put at-Suppress-Warnings "unchecked", with a note, right on that one array declaration. A repo-wide, or class-wide, suppression hides the next, real warning too — so keep it tight.

Finally, varargs plus generics. A generic varargs method — like a list-of method taking T-dot-dot-dot items — quietly creates a T array under the hood. That's the same forbidden array, so the compiler warns about possible heap pollution. If your method only reads the varargs — never storing the array, never leaking it — then the operation is safe, and you certify that by putting at-Safe-Varargs on the method.

So here's the one-line summary. Every gotcha here is the same collision: arrays keep their type at run time, generics erase theirs. Keep them apart — favour a list of T over a T array, treat "unchecked" as a claim you must be able to justify, and suppress it narrowly, and mark read-only generic varargs at-Safe-Varargs. Do that, and the sharp edges stop cutting. With the mechanics complete, the final section steps back to designing generic APIs well.
